now tell me what will happen if i create a super admin and give him access to add product card and upload pics

Currently, the app does not have real authentication. The admin upload page and /api/upload endpoint are publicly accessible, though uploads are limited and validated.
If you add a Super Admin system, the admin should be able to:
Log in securely.
Add, edit, reorder, and remove product cards.
Upload and replace product images.
Manage categories and product details.
Access protected admin-only pages and APIs.
Important protections are required:
Password hashing, never plain-text passwords.
Secure sessions or JWTs with expiration.
Role-based access control.
Protected server-side API routes—not only hidden UI buttons.
CSRF protection and upload rate limiting.
Audit logs for product and image changes.
Secure image validation and storage.
The public website will still show the products, but visitors will not be able to modify them. Also, a Super Admin cannot hide client-side website code completely; they only control protected management functions.

For a Super Admin who can add products and upload images, you will need a backend and persistent storage.
The public website can remain mostly static for speed, while the admin side becomes dynamic:
Database: products, categories, users, roles.
Authentication: Super Admin login and sessions.
Storage: product images in cloud storage or a server-managed upload system.
API: protected endpoints for creating and editing products.
Your current upload system writes files locally, which is suitable for development but unreliable after deployment. A practical setup would be:
Next.js frontend
PostgreSQL or Supabase database
Supabase Auth or another authentication provider
Supabase Storage or Cloudinary for images
So the site can be a hybrid: static public pages with a dynamic backend/admin panel.